icked him his name is takayev it  left him in a really weak position so as  protests spiraled out of control takiev  didn't really have another choice he had  to call in those russian troops because  he did not have the backing of the  security services or the kazakh military  so that's why this is such a big deal  you mentioned that there have been two  leaders and of course there's only been  two leaders since the nation turned  independent how would you compare the  level of corruption cronyism in  kazakhstan versus other countries in the  region  it's high kazakhstan is a is a wealthy  it's it's a very wealthy country because  of its uh because of its natural  resources and corruption is very high  rferl radio for your uh radio for  european liberty has done some excellent  expose into the uh corruption  surrounding nazarbayev and his regime  and people knew about it right so that's  that's part of the the real legitimate  anger that we're seeing but so there was  a legitimate protest movement that got  out of control but i i think it's it's  at this point  a lot is not known because the internet  keeps going in and out and people can't  freely go in and out of the country  right now but if if you have to ask me  to summarize what's happening it looks  like it's a coup  by takaya against nazarbayev it looks  like this is the end of the nazarbayev  era in kazakhstan there are reports that  the nazir by a family has fled  kazakhstan we don't know if those are  true or not but that's definitely  something to watch takayev had to call  in uh he had to call in russia to bring  uh forces in the collective security and  treaty organization they've never  deployed forces before and this is a  complete change in kazakhstan's foreign  policy they had something called a  multi-vector foreign policy they were  always trying to balance interests but  now  they're they're they're they're going to  be very reliant on russia as a result of  this so it's clear why kazakhstan and  why takay have called in russia but what  does russia get out of lending military  support to quash these protests inside  of kazakhstan  it's a great day for moscow honestly  moscow doesn't like instability uh but  they came out on top  as far as it looks now so it what what  it did is it told kazakhstan's security  services to get behind takayev that  takaya passed moscow's backing and it  also allows moscow to prop up a leader  who's domestically unpopular and it's  cheap while reinforcing the point to all  of the neighbors that if they step out  of line uh that they're next  but it says something even i think more  sinister and and creepy it says that  moscow has a weak guy in charge who owes  them i don't think you're gonna call in  the favor right away but he owes them  right it's a check that they can cash  later on  china of course also has their strategic  interest here in kazakhstan because they  share a border and they're important  trading partners because of china's one  belt one road infrastructure initiative  chinese president xi jinping actually  warned it opposes any opposing forces  foreign forces that are plotting color  revolution who do you think xi jinping  is referring to here  so  it's a weird quote right uh yesterday in  one of the major moscow papers the uh  the russians said that they had put  together a color revolution in order to  distract from the major security talks  between russia and the west so moscow is  very good at saying there's a hidden  hand yesterday moscow blamed the state  department for the unrest in kazakhstan  china is basically  saying it's signaling by making this  statement that it's a-okay with what  moscow is doing in kazakhstan moscow  provides the muscle the forces on the  ground and beijing provides the capital  and this is this is a really i think a  scary a formula that could be repeated  elsewhere so this is definitely  something to watch  that is really interesting the way you  frame that russia and how w